    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park/Casual-Academic-Register---College-of-Humanities--Arts-and-Social-Sciences_JR0000000090": {
      "title": "Casual Academic Register – College of Humanities, Arts and Social Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2024-11-18\nPosted: Posted 30+ Days Ago\nPart time\n\nAbout Flinders Our bold vision, captured in our Strategic Plan: Making a Difference: The 2025 Agenda, is to be internationally recognised as a world leader in research, an innovator in contemporary education, and the source of Australia’s most enterprising graduates. To realise this ambition, we recently made a significant organisational change to a six College structure with a professional staff and services alignment. We recognise the key to our success is exceptional people and we’re seeking an outstanding individual to join the leadership team of our transformed university. Casual Register Flinders University has a number of Casual Registers as a means of expressions of interest for the purpose of casual work, should such opportunities arise. How to Register: Upload your current CV/Resumé detailing your qualifications and experience. You may choose to upload a Cover Letter of no more than 1 page to support your CV/Resumé. Your application will be acknowledged by an automated e-mail once submitted. If a casual opportunity is available and your application is considered suitable, the relevant hiring manager will contact you for a further discussion. Please note, only suitable applicants will be contacted. Applying to the Casual Register does not guarantee employment. Further Information: Pursuant to Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA) this position may be deemed prescribed.  It is an inherent requirement of a prescribed position that the successful candidate maintains a current Working With Children Check which is satisfactory to the University. Please contact Recruitment@Flinders  by email at recruitment@flinders.edu.au  should you have any queries regarding Casual opportunities."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Alumni-and-Advancement-Operations-Officer_JR0000017539": {
      "title": "Alumni and Advancement Operations Officer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-14\nPosted: Posted Today\nFull time\n\nPosition Details   Full-Time | Fixed term (2 years)  Higher Education Officer Level 4 | $76,140 - $82,119  Location:  Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description About the Role   The Alumni and Advancement Operations Officer provides administrative support to help the University maintain accurate alumni and donor records, coordinate activities and events, and support the day-to-day operations of the Alumni and Advancement team. This is an excellent opportunity for an organised administrator to gain exposure to alumni relations, fundraising operations and stakeholder engagement within a collaborative university environment.   Key Responsibilities include: Maintaining accurate alumni, donor and stakeholder records in Raiser’s Edge. Supporting data entry, record updates, gift processing and routine data maintenance activities. Providing administrative support including meeting coordination, minute taking, document preparation and responding to enquiries. Assisting with alumni events and engagement activities, including registrations, logistics and record management. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 through supporting alumni engagement activities, donor relationships and the effective management of alumni and advancement data. The role reflects Here for Good, Stronger Together, Innovation and Excellence through collaboration, quality service, accurate data management and continuous improvement. About You   You are a highly organised and detail-oriented professional with strong administrative capabilities and a passion for delivering excellent service. You enjoy working across a variety of tasks, managing competing priorities, and contributing to meaningful stakeholder engagement outcomes within a collaborative team environment. You will also bring: Experience using CRM or database systems, with Raiser's Edge experience highly regarded. Strong written and verbal communication skills and a collaborative approach to working with others. Proficiency in Microsoft Office applications, particularly Word and Excel. The ability to manage multiple tasks, maintain attention to detail, and meet deadlines. About the Office of Alumni and Advancement    The Alumni Relations team is committed to actively engaging our graduates in the life of the University through meaningful programs, benefits, services, and communications. The Alumni team also help facilitate class reunions, as well as provide opportunities for professional development, mentoring and presenting. The Advancement team initiate and develop fundraising activities for the University, due to the strong support from its community of donors. Life at Flinders      They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.    Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.    Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.     So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment    Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan     Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment    Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. This includes: A current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.  How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a cover letter as a separate document, telling us a little about yourself, and why you feel you are suited to this role.  Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through  recruitment@flinders.edu.au  or call 08 8201 2383.  For more information regarding this position, please contact  Paul Robertson Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 27 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Campus-and-Space-Planner_JR0000017523": {
      "title": "Campus and Space Planner",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details   Full-Time | Continuing  Higher Education Officer Level 6 | $94,456 - $100,710  Location:  Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description About the Role   Join Flinders University's Property, Facilities and Development team and play a key role in shaping the future of the University's campuses. Reporting to the Strategic Planning Lead, the Campus and Space Planner contributes to a broad range of planning initiatives, supporting the development of engaging, functional and sustainable campus environments through strategic planning, space management and stakeholder engagement.    Other key responsibilities include:  Support campus planning projects across campus, space, transport, placemaking and retail initiatives.   Conduct research, analysis and reporting to inform strategic planning decisions.  Assist with space reviews, audits, wayfinding, and planning proposals to optimise campus environments.  Collaborate with stakeholders and colleagues to maintain planning data, records and documentation in line with University standards.    For more information on the key position responsibilities, please see the  position description .  This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to “Changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters”, by 2035 through supporting the planning and development of campus environments that enable outstanding student and staff experiences. Through strategic planning, collaboration and continuous improvement, the role advances Flinders' commitment to being student-centric, innovative and excellent in the delivery of its spaces and places.  About You   You are a highly organised and analytical planning professional with strong stakeholder engagement skills and a keen eye for detail. Comfortable working both independently and collaboratively, you enjoy solving problems, managing competing priorities and contributing to projects that enhance the experience and functionality of university spaces.     To thrive in this role, you will demonstrate:  Relevant qualifications or experience in interior architecture, urban planning and design, data analytics or a related field.  Strong communication and stakeholder management skills with a customer-focused approach.  Excellent organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities with accuracy.  Experience interpreting data and information to support planning and decision-making outcomes.    While experience in campus and space planning is highly regarded, we are equally interested in candidates who demonstrate strong analytical, stakeholder engagement and problem-solving skills. If you have the right mindset and a willingness to learn, we'll provide support and development to help you succeed in the role.  About Property, Facilities and Development      The Property, Facilities and Development (PFD) team works across all of Flinders’ places, spaces, and physical infrastructure. Working across four five areas: Strategic Planning; Sustainability; Capital Development; Customer and Site Services; and Strategic Projects, we are responsible for the planning, development, capital delivery, management, and maintenance of university spaces to ensure they are fit for purpose and support Flinders to deliver its strategic objectives.   Life at Flinders      They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.     Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.     Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.     Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.      So,  why work at Flinders?    17% Superannuation + salary packaging options    Flexible working arrangements    Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program   Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers     We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.    Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).    Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice        Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment      Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.    Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan      Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy         Prescribed Conditions for Employment      Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. This includes:   A current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role.    How to Apply and Information   You are required to submit a CV, along with a cover letter as a separate document, telling us about yourself, your skills and experience, and why you are interested in joining Flinders University. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through  recruitment@flinders.edu.au  or call 08 8201 2383.  For more information regarding this position, please contact  Hannah Kennedy Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 27 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Flinders-Medical-Centre--Kaurna-Country/Coordinator--Enrolment-and-Student-Progress--Doctor-of-Medicine-_JR0000017520": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Enrolment and Student Progress",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-11\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Fixed Term until 30 June 2027 Higher Education Officer Level 7 | $102,589 - $113,561 Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country   View Position Description About the Role   Due to an internal promotion, an exciting opportunity is available for a Coordinator, Enrolment and Student Progress to join the Student Administration Services (SAS) team based in Flinders Medical Centre at Bedford Park. Working at the College level, you will play a key role in coordinating high-quality enrolment and student progress services, supporting students and staff across a broad range of student administration activities. As a trusted subject matter expert, you will provide specialist advice, manage complex cases, and contribute to initiatives that enhance the student experience and support student success.   Working closely with the College Manager, Student Administration Services and the Coordinator, Enrolment and Student Progress (Doctor of Medicine), you will contribute to strategic initiatives, support continuous improvement activities, and help review processes and systems to ensure the delivery of effective, student-centred services across the University.   As the Coordinator, Enrolment and Student Progress, you will be responsible for: Providing expert advice on enrolment, course planning and student progress matters to staff, students and key stakeholders.  Coordinating the delivery of timely, consistent and high-quality enrolment and student progress services, including resolving complex enquiries and cases.  Supporting university-wide enrolment activities and student success initiatives that enhance the student experience and retention outcomes. Driving continuous improvement through the review of processes, systems and service delivery practices. This position makes a significant contribution to the University’s strategic plan to changing 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035 by supporting students at critical stages of their academic journey and helping to deliver a positive and engaging student experience. Through collaboration with colleagues and stakeholders across the University, the role reflects Flinders' commitment to being stronger together and student centred, ensuring students receive the guidance, support and services they need to succeed. About You   You thrive in a fast-paced and evolving environment and enjoy balancing operational responsibilities with process improvement initiatives. With strong organisational and problem-solving skills, you can manage competing priorities, navigate complex matters and deliver high-quality outcomes. You are a collaborative professional who builds positive relationships with colleagues and stakeholders and provides sound advice with confidence and professionalism.   To be successful in this role, you will demonstrate: Experience managing support services within a large, complex and dynamic organisation, including interpreting, applying and providing advice and recommendations on higher education policies and procedures to senior stakeholders. Proven ability to exercise initiative and judgement, communicate decisions effectively, and manage competing priorities to meet critical deadlines. High-level organisational, analytical and communication skills, with the ability to prepare detailed reports, submissions and recommendations for a range of audiences. The ability to provide leadership and contribute to a positive team culture that supports customer service excellence, collaboration and continuous improvement. About the Student Administration Services   Student Administration Services operates under a university wide matrix services model. SAS teams are integrated within each of the five colleges, along with the front-end student service hub (Flinders Connect) and university-wide administration services to provide support across the student lifecycle, from admissions through to graduations. The suite of services that the SAS unit delivers are crucial to the student experience. These services act as the scaffold around the learning experience. In a world of increasing choices and options for students, service excellence is non-negotiable. In SAS, we understand that improving the student experience requires a holistic, one team approach. The SAS team also likes to have fun and we celebrate our successes. Although spread across colleges and campuses, we take time to come together and build relationships across teams. Social activities, training and development sessions, communities of practice and staff led projects support engagement and provide opportunities to extend our understanding of the University and its environment. We make administration fun! Life at Flinders   They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.    Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.    Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create.    Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.     So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment      Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies.   Please see here for our  Reconciliation Action Plan     Please see here for our  Indigenous Workforce Strategy     Prescribed Conditions for Employment   Flinders University is committed to providing a safe, respectful and inclusive environment, free from gender‑based violence. Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre‑employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. This includes a Gender‑Based Violence declaration, in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender‑Based Violence (2025), and a Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check. How to Apply and Information   As well as a CV, you are required to submit a Suitability Statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the below Key Position Capabilities of the position description : Experience in managing the provision of support services in a large, diverse, and dynamic work environment, and to provide advice and recommendation on strategic matters to senior management. Demonstrated ability to exercise initiative and independent judgement, establish and manage competing priorities and complex workflows with non-negotiable deadlines. High level organisational, planning and analytical skills, including excellent attention to detail and ability to prepare meaningful and detailed reports and submissions. Demonstrated ability to identify and lead continuous process improvement activities and ability to evaluate administrative systems and procedures and recommend innovative improvements where appropriate. Ability to provide leadership and contribute to a workplace culture that motivates, guides and supports staff to deliver customer service excellence and business improvement. Highly developed communication and interpersonal skills, to be effective with oral presentations, persuasion, and negotiation, including abilities in dealing with sensitive matters. Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Kathryn Lowry . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 27 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Senior-Lecturer---Associate-Professor-in-Law_JR0000017514": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor in Law",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full Time I Continuing Academic Level C I AU$141,370 - $162,619 pa | 17% Super Academic Level D I AU$169,706 - $186,706 pa | 17% Super Senior Lecturer Position Description | Associate Professor Position Description Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country Shape the future of Law at Flinders: Join a growing and ambitious Law discipline recognised for the strength of its academic expertise, innovative legal education and commitment to research that makes a difference. Flinders University is seeking an accomplished Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor in Law (Teaching and Research) to provide academic leadership and make a significant contribution to the future direction of the discipline. You will bring an established research profile, academic leadership and a passion for developing the next generation of legal professionals, while building meaningful connections across the legal profession, government, industry and community. Current teaching needs include areas such as Evidence, Health Law, Torts, Administrative Law, and Public Law. Your research will contribute to one of Flinders’ strategic research priorities, with opportunities to collaborate across leading research centres including the Jeff Bleich Centre and Centre for Social Impact . You will: Lead innovative legal education, shaping curriculum, coordinating topics and courses, and enhancing the student experience Drive research excellence and impact through high-quality publications, competitive grants and research collaboration Mentor and lead others, including early career academics, research students and high-performing teaching and research teams Build influential partnerships with industry, professional and community stakeholders nationally and internationally About You: You are an established legal academic with a strong reputation for research, teaching and academic leadership. You will have a demonstrated record of research achievement appropriate to your career stage, alongside the ability to inspire students, colleagues and research collaborators. You will bring the following, aligned with your career stage: A PhD in Law or equivalent qualification, with significant tertiary teaching experience A strong research track record, including high-quality publications and success securing competitive external funding Evidence of tertiary teaching excellence, including topic and course coordination, and curriculum design, including the ability to teach and assess practical legal skills. Demonstrated academic leadership, including curriculum development, course or topic coordination, supervision and mentoring Strong national and/or international networks, with the ability to develop partnerships that create meaningful teaching and research opportunities About the College of Business, Creative Arts, Law and Social Sciences The College of Business, Creative Arts, Law and Social Sciences delivers innovative and socially impactful education across a diverse range of disciplines. Our Law programs are recognised for their practical focus, commitment to social justice and strong engagement with industry and community. The College’s mission is to embrace innovative knowledge and practice to tackle the challenges of our time, with a strategic focus on technology, health and social impact. We are committed to developing career-ready graduates equipped with the knowledge, skills and critical thinking capabilities to succeed in a rapidly evolving legal landscape. Why Flinders University? They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here.  Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next.  Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them.   So,  why work at Flinders?   17% Superannuation + salary packaging options   Flexible working arrangements   Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our  Thriving@Flinders  staff wellbeing program  Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers    We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards.   Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre  (Bedford Park, South Australia).   Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice    Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is committed to reconciliation and strongly encourages applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples. We are dedicated to creating an inclusive workplace that supports Indigenous advancement in education, employment, research and wellbeing. Prescribed Conditions for Employment A current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check satisfactory to the University will be required before the successful applicant can commence in this position. Appointment is also conditional upon completion of a Gender Based Violence declaration in accordance with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender Based Violence (2025). International candidates may be considered where they hold an Australian law degree or PhD, or have relevant legal practice experience in Australia that meets the requirements of the position. Relocation support is available to the successful candidate. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit your CV and a Suitability Statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the Key Position Capabilities of the relevant position description: Position Description I Senior Lecturer in Law Position Description I Associate Professor in Law For more information regarding this position, please contact Associate Professor Sam Kontra Applications to be submitted before 10:00 pm (ACDT) on Friday, 9 October 2026. Please note, late applications and applications sent via agencies will not be accepted . At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 09 Oct 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
