    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Tonsley/Industry-Partnerships-Manager--Engineering-and-Computer-Sciences-_JR0000017513": {
      "title": "Industry Partnerships Manager (Engineering and Computer Sciences)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Continuing I Full-Time Higher Education Officer Level 9 I $133,860- $143,239 pa | 17% Superannuation Location: Tonsley Campus , Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Opportunity We are seeking an experienced and commercially minded Industry Partnerships Manager to build strategic connections between the College of Science and Engineering at Flinders University and industry, government and community partners. This is a highly connected and influential role, where you will identify opportunities, bring the right people together and turn ideas into meaningful collaborations across engineering and computer science. With a particular focus on growth areas including defence, sustainable energy, artificial intelligence and cybersecurity , you will help position the College as a partner of choice for industry engagement, innovation and talent. In this role, you will: Build and grow strategic partnerships with industry, government, research and community stakeholders, aligned to the College’s teaching, research and innovation capabilities. Connect industry with Flinders expertise to develop collaborative projects, research opportunities, commercial activities and new funding opportunities. Expand industry-connected learning through placements, FUSECo projects, Industry Honours and Masters projects, Enterprise PhDs and other industry opportunities. Lead and grow industry engagement capability by managing and developing a team, strengthening systems and processes, and providing strategic advice to College leadership and academic teams. This position is open to candidates with Australian Work Rights. Relocation support is available. About You: You are a strategic relationship builder who thrives at the intersection of industry, education, research and innovation. You can identify opportunities, build trusted relationships and turn ideas into partnerships that deliver meaningful outcomes. You will bring the commercial acumen and sector knowledge to engage confidently with industry, government and academic stakeholders and connect complementary expertise to create new opportunities. To be successful in this role, you will bring: Extensive industry partnership and business development experience, with a demonstrated ability to translate opportunities into strategic partnerships, projects and funding outcomes. Strong knowledge of engineering and/or computing, with the ability to engage credibly with technical and academic stakeholders and understand emerging industry and workforce needs. Highly developed leadership and stakeholder management skills, including the ability to lead teams, influence senior stakeholders and facilitate collaboration across diverse organisations. Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. So,   why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Wide range of professional development activities and services We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy Prescribed Conditions for Employment A current Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check which is satisfactory to the University will be required by Flinders University before the successful applicant can commence in this position. A criminal record will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. This position may require the successful applicant to hold (or be eligible to obtain) an NV1 (Negative Vetting Level 1) Security Clearance. How to Apply and Information As well as a CV, you are required to submit a suitability statement of no more than 3 pages, addressing the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . For more information regarding this position, we strongly encourage you to contact Clint Chapman .   Applications to be submitted before 10:00 pm (ACST) on Wednesday, 30 September 2026. Please note, late applications and applications sent via agencies will not be accepted . At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 30 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Associate-Lecturer-in-Paramedicine--Clinical-Facilitator-_JR0000017510": {
      "title": "Associate Lecturer in Paramedicine (Clinical Facilitator)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-10\nPosted: Posted 4 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Continuing Position Academic Level A | $90,930 - $110,197 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country View Position Description About the Role Join our Paramedicine team as an Associate Lecturer (Clinical Facilitator), where you will play a pivotal role in delivering high-quality work-integrated learning (WIL) experiences that prepare students to become confident, capable and career-ready paramedicine professionals. Working collaboratively with academic staff, healthcare partners and students, you will coordinate and support clinical placements, foster strong industry relationships, and contribute to the continuous improvement of clinical education programs. This position combines student support, stakeholder engagement and quality assurance to ensure meaningful, safe and authentic learning experiences. Key responsibilities include: Coordinate and support student placements across a range of healthcare and clinical settings, ensuring effective scheduling, compliance, record management and placement administration. Monitor and support student learning, clinical competency development and professional progression through coaching, feedback, assessment support and pastoral care. Build and maintain strong partnerships with healthcare organisations, clinical supervisors and placement providers to enhance placement quality, capacity and student outcomes. Contribute to quality assurance, accreditation and continuous improvement initiatives, supporting innovation and excellence in clinical education and work-integrated learning. This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan to change 10 million lives, Every One Matters, by 2035, through directly contributing to the strategic drivers of Student Centricity and Industry & Enterprise Partnerships. By fostering high-quality clinical learning experiences, supporting student success and wellbeing, and building strong partnerships with healthcare organisations, this role helps prepare capable, career-ready graduates while strengthening the University's impact across the communities and industries it serves. About You You are a passionate and collaborative paramedicine professional with experience supporting student learning in clinical or work-integrated learning environments. You bring strong communication and relationship-building skills, a commitment to student success, and the ability to work effectively with a broad range of stakeholders to deliver positive educational outcomes. Organised and proactive, you are comfortable balancing placement coordination, student support and quality assurance activities while contributing to continuous improvement initiatives. You will bring: A relevant degree qualification and contemporary clinical experience, together with experience in clinical education, supervision or work-integrated learning environments. A demonstrated ability to mentor and support students, fostering professional growth, clinical competence and positive learning experiences. Strong stakeholder engagement, communication and interpersonal skills, with a demonstrated ability to build productive relationships across academia, healthcare organisations and industry. Excellent organisational and administrative capabilities, including an understanding of compliance, accreditation, risk management and the use of digital systems to support placement and student management. College of Medicine and Public Health            At the College of Medicine and Public Health, we deliver clinical, population and lab-based research, integrated teaching programs and high-quality clinical services. Our footprint reaches out from our world class teaching hospital at the Flinders Medical Centre in South Australia to multiple rural clinical locations all the way to Darwin in the Northern Territory. Our research and teaching equip the next generation of leaders and innovators with the skill, commitment, and vision to protect vulnerable communities and truly advance health outcomes. It is at the heart of everything we do. Life at Flinders They say know your place. We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy. It is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA). How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Andrew Makkink . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 08 Oct 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Flinders-Rural-Health-SA-Nuriootpa-Barossa--Ngadjuri-Country/Senior-Project-Officer---Education-Pathways_JR0000017502": {
      "title": "Senior Project Officer – Education Pathways",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Fixed Term – 2 Years | Full-time Higher Education Officer Level 7 | $102,589 - $113,561 p.a. Location: Any RRH SA or NT Campus View Position Description About the Role As the Senior Project Officer - Education Pathways, you will play a key role in developing, implementing and strengthening pathways into the Flinders Medical Program, with a particular focus on the Indigenous Entry Stream and Rural Entry Pathway. Working closely with academic leaders, professional staff and external stakeholders, you will lead strategic education projects, coordinate complex program activities, and drive initiatives that support increased participation and success for students from rural, remote and Indigenous communities. This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to meaningful educational outcomes and workforce development across South Australia and the Northern Territory. This position can be based at any Flinders Rural and Remote campus across South Australia or the Northern Territory. Key responsibilities include: Lead and coordinate the delivery, enhancement and ongoing success of the Indigenous Entry Stream and Rural Entry Pathway programs across multiple campuses and locations. Manage end-to-end program operations, including participant engagement, applications, selection processes, enrolments, reporting and stakeholder communications. Plan and deliver strategic education projects, monitor outcomes, manage risks and provide high-level advice to support informed decision-making and continuous improvement. Build and maintain strong partnerships with students, academic staff, health services, community organisations and other key stakeholders to achieve sustainable program outcomes. This position makes a significant contribution to the University's strategic plan, Changing Lives, Every One Matters , by 2035 through directly contributing to our Stronger Together core enabler. By fostering collaborative partnerships with students, communities, health services and academic stakeholders, the role supports inclusive education pathways and sustainable workforce outcomes across rural and remote Australia. About You You are a highly organised and collaborative project professional with experience delivering complex programs and initiatives in a higher education, health, community or related environment. You bring strong project management, stakeholder engagement and problem-solving skills, along with a commitment to improving educational opportunities and outcomes for diverse communities. Comfortable working independently and as part of a collaborative team, you thrive in dynamic environments where you can make a meaningful impact. To be successful in this role, you will have: Demonstrated experience in project planning, implementation, monitoring and evaluation, with the ability to deliver outcomes within set timeframes and priorities. Strong organisational, analytical and problem-solving skills, with the ability to identify improvements and develop practical solutions. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare high-quality reports, briefs and stakeholder communications. Proven ability to build productive relationships and work effectively with a broad range of stakeholders, including people from diverse cultural and community backgrounds. Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT At Flinders Rural and Remote Health NT , our aim is to be a hub for innovative health research, education, and workforce development across the Northern Territory.  We engage with students, community, and stakeholders to provide critical programs, such as the Northern Territory Medical Program, Paramedicine, Rural Clinical School, and the University Department of Rural Health. Our postgraduate education pathways (Remote Health Practice program), provides workforce development through Indigenous health and clinical skills training, and we have a community driven research program led by researchers committed to improving health outcomes. We work alongside Poche SA+NT focussing on Indigenous leadership capacity development and developing the Indigenous health workforce.  All activities are driven by our values which focus on the long-term and short-term needs of the NT community. Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA Flinders Rural and Remote Health SA (FRRHSA) facilitates high quality multidisciplinary health education and research in our State's regional areas. We have a strong national and international reputation for providing community-engaged inter-professional training for medical, nursing, and allied health students. This includes the SA Rural Medical (SARM) program and multidisciplinary rural clinical placements. Through this practice, we bring new and exciting opportunities to actively engage and work with rural communities.  Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy. Staff working in a health care setting are required to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the NT Health policy. It is a requirement of this position that the incumbent maintain a current Working with Children Check which is satisfactory to the University in accordance with the Child Safety (Prohibited Persons) Act 2016 (SA). Northern Territory Government legislation (the Care and Protection of Children Act 2007) now requires all individuals in child related employment or whose duties involve, or are likely to involve, contact with a child, to hold a valid 'Working with Children Clearance' (Ochre Card). This position involves child related work and is assessed as requiring a Working with Children Clearance/Ochre Card. How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages and address the Key Position Capabilities of the position description . Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please contact the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact Claire Drummond . Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 23 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
    },
    "https://flinders.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/flinders_employment/job/Bedford-Park--Kaurna-Country/Statistician_JR0000017501": {
      "title": "Statistician",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-09\nPosted: Posted 5 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition Details Full-Time | Fixed term Academic Level A | $90,930 - $110,197 p.a. Location: Bedford Park / Kaurna Country/ Adelaide CBD (SAHMRI) View Position Description About the Role As a Statistician supporting Registry of Senior Australians Research Centre’s core goals, the incumbent will lead and contribute to high-quality, impactful research focused on aged care and health services for older people. Working across multidisciplinary research teams, the Statistician will play a key role in delivering research that supports the Centre’s strategic priorities and contributes to improving outcomes for older Australians. The role will work with a range of large and complex datasets, including aged care data, Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme (PBS) and Medicare Benefits Schedule (MBS) data, hospitalisation records, and Department of Social Services Data on Multiple Individual Occurrences (DOMINO) data. The role is also responsible for: Assist in undertaking and coordinating statistical analyses, including complex multivariable modelling, and survival analysis. Undertake studies regarding quality and safety monitoring, variation in care quality, and value-based care. Assist in leading and coordinating research projects needs through their lifecycles. This may include advising and monitoring project timelines, milestones, and deliverables. Collaborating with the analytical team data management, linkage efforts, and quality control processes when necessary for ROSA’s implementation, performance enhancement, and other areas as needed. About You You have experience in quantitative research methodologies and a commitment to delivering high-quality research outcomes. You have a demonstrated ability to manage research activities effectively and deliver project milestones and targets within agreed timeframes. You bring well-developed interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build positive relationships, collaborate effectively, and engage confidently with a diverse range of stakeholders, colleagues, and industry partners. Your ability to contribute effectively to collaborative research projects, establish and maintain productive professional relationships, and work constructively as part of a team, with a demonstrated initiative and capacity to work independently, while recognising when to seek guidance, support, or expertise when required. Excellent written and oral communication skills within an academic or research environment are essential. You will also be able to demonstrate: A minimum satisfactory completion of 4 years tertiary study or equivalent qualifications and experience in a relevant field and/or area of research. Excellent proficiency working with at least one software package (i.e., Stata, SAS or R). Experience working with observational data, multivariable modelling, confounding/interaction issues, survival analysis, data visualisation. Emerging research experience in terms of publications and presentations at a national and/or international level in a relevant field. College of Health and Enablement  At the College of Health and Enablement, we aim to be an innovator in contemporary education producing a safer and more resilient health and social care workforce. Our mission is to improve lives and empower communities, enabling better health and caring in the world through an exceptional student learning experience. At the College, our research mission is about co-creating new knowledge which promotes wellbeing, and better care and support, to individuals and communities across the lifespan. We work proactively with industry partners and professional bodies leading to more employable and innovative graduates.  Life at Flinders They say know your place.  We say you've found it right here. Flinders University was founded on the belief that learning should open minds, celebrate individuality and give people the freedom to pursue their own point of view. That spirit lives on in our community of thinkers, doers and changemakers who challenge convention, and each other, to shape what comes next. Rising to a challenge is nothing new for Flinders. During our first 60 years, we’ve navigated revolutionary changes in social and cultural norms, and have responded to transformative economic, environmental and technological developments. Through it all, we’ve continued to deliver for our students and community, innovating in learning and research to prepare graduates for the changing world they help create. Reaching beyond the limits of buildings, borders, and backgrounds, ours is an inclusive culture that believes absolutely in equality and opportunity for all. We don't just accommodate differences; we embrace and celebrate them. So,  why work at Flinders? 17% Superannuation + salary packaging options Flexible working arrangements Awarded the AHEIA Higher Education Award for Workplace Culture Excellence due to our commitment to wellbeing through our Thriving@Flinders staff wellbeing program Wide range of professional development activities and services, including exclusive staff study offers  We embrace diversity and promote equity and inclusion for all students and staff, as shown through our three SAGE Cygnet Awards. Vibrant campus life and amenities including on campus  health care services ,  gym  and  childcare centre (Bedford Park, South Australia). Recognised as a Veteran Employer of Choice.  Our Commitment to Reconciliation and Indigenous Employment Flinders University is proud to be an organisation that is committed to our Reconciliation Action Plan and Indigenous Workforce Strategy. Our vision is to be a preferred employer for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples.  We are committed to progressing Indigenous advancement in education, research, employment, and wellbeing, and strongly encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples for all Flinders vacancies. Please see here for our Reconciliation Action Plan   Please see here for our Indigenous Workforce Strategy   Prescribed Conditions for Employment Appointment to this role is conditional on the completion of required pre-employment checks and declarations, each of which must be satisfactory to the University. These include: Flinders University will require a current completed Nationally Coordinated Criminal History Check and Gender Based Violence Declaration which is satisfactory to the University. The contract of Employment is conditional upon the receipt of these completed compliance checks. A disclosable outcome will not automatically disqualify a candidate from consideration. Each case will be assessed on its individual merits and relevance to the inherent requirements of the role. SA: Staff working in a health care setting are strongly recommended to be fully vaccinated against COVID-19 in line with the SA Health policy How to Apply and Information You are required to submit a CV, along with a Suitability Statement as a separate document.  This should be no more than 3 pages, and address the Key Position Capabilities of the Position Description Flinders University is committed to providing an environment where staff with disability are valued, supported and encouraged to participate fully and independently in the life of the University. If you require any reasonable adjustments to assist you in the selection process, please advise the contact in the Recruitment Team so that we can make appropriate arrangements through recruitment@flinders.edu.au or call 08 8201 2383. For more information regarding this position, please contact johannes.schwabe@flinders.edu.au Certification Applications to be submitted before 10.00pm: 30 Sept 2026 At Flinders we embrace and celebrate diversity and encourage applications from Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, and people of all ages, ethnicities, abilities, sexual orientations, and gender identities. Flinders. Fearless."
